01. EBS
EBS = Elastic Block Store

This is persistent block storage attached to EC2.

Think:

EC2
 |
 +---- EBS Volume

Example:

EC2
 ├── Root EBS
 │    └── OS
 │
 └── Additional EBS
      └── Application data

Important distinction:

EC2 = compute
EBS = block storage



02. EBS vs Instance Store
Very important interview topic.

EBS

Persistent.

EC2
 ↓
Stop instance
 ↓
EBS data remains
Instance Store

Ephemeral.

EC2
 ↓
Instance termination
 ↓
Instance-store data lost

So don't use instance store for data that must survive instance termination.



03. EBS Volume Types
Common ones:

gp3
io2
st1
sc1

For most normal applications:

gp3

is a common choice.

For high-performance databases or workloads requiring very high IOPS:

io2

may be appropriate.



04. Snapshots:
EBS snapshot = backup of an EBS volume.

Example:

EBS
 ↓
Snapshot
 ↓
S3-backed AWS storage

You can use snapshots for:

Backup
Disaster recovery
Creating new volumes
AMI creation



05. Security Groups:
Security Group = virtual firewall for EC2.

Example:

Internet
   |
   | TCP 443
   ↓
Security Group
   |
   ↓
EC2

Rules:

Inbound
22   SSH
80   HTTP
443  HTTPS
8080 Application

But production best practice is not:

0.0.0.0/0 → SSH 22

Instead, restrict SSH access where possible.



06. Security Group is Stateful
Suppose:

Client → EC2
       TCP 443

If allowed inbound traffic creates a connection, return traffic is automatically allowed by the stateful behavior of the security group.

This differs from a stateless network ACL.

Remember:

Security Group  → Stateful
NACL            → Stateless



07. Key Pair
Used for SSH authentication to Linux EC2 instances.

Example:

ssh -i mykey.pem ec2-user@<public-ip>

You should understand:

Key Pair
   ↓
Private key (.pem)
   ↓
SSH
   ↓
EC2

Never commit .pem files into Git.

Bad:

GitHub
 └── production-key.pem
